 $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x+3)y & =(x+3){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b6627