 "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{′} & =2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form ${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$ for some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-342276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "789c99d7502ef879b7255a15dee3a03e12b61fbf0863058a3878a07030aca7d4"} {"question" "q-342276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-342276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +1=0"] ["math" "2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0"] ["math" "4{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0"] ["math" "{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1=0"] ["math" "{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-342276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(6x-1){y}^{′}-3y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "789c99d7502ef879b7255a15dee3a03e12b61fbf0863058a3878a07030aca7d4"} {"question" "q-345399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+12x{y}^{′}+(9x-2)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +12\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "789c99d7502ef879b7255a15dee3a03e12b61fbf0863058a3878a07030aca7d4"} {"question" "q-345279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n10x{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 7{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 7{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +7\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (10\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "789c99d7502ef879b7255a15dee3a03e12b61fbf0863058a3878a07030aca7d4"} {"question" "q-345430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/10}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/5}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-3/10}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-3/5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(6x+1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x+1){y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x+1){y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "789c99d7502ef879b7255a15dee3a03e12b61fbf0863058a3878a07030aca7d4"} {"question" "q-345432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n/2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221