icial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4{y}^{′} & =-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 3y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -4\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (3\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "789c99d7502ef879b7255a15dee3a03e12b61fbf0863058a3878a07030aca7d4"} {"question" "q-345407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-5/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "q-345407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/state.json" "file_sha256" "a3022aa473daf24c57e93b662799a102eabde7b221c3bb6450b6be0a7f70fb35"} {"question" "e-21851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx^2y'' + x(x-3)y' - 6y = 0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/example-21851.json" "file_sha256" "bdfcfcb41977732ee2a20a099da23f33103daa5e520b5fa91d68b7bc6b293816"} {"question" "e-21851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n= \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives $y'$ and $y''{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(x-3){y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}-3x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y'$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(x-3)y'\n=\n\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty (\\lambda+m-1)a_{m-1}x^{\\lambda+m}\n-\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 3(\\lambda+n)a_nx^{\\lambda+n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -3\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n\\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/example-21851.json" "file_sha256" "bdfcfcb41977732ee2a20a099da23f33103daa5e520b5fa91d68b7bc6b293816"} {"question" "e-21897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n12x^2y'' + 7xy' + (2x-3)y = 0\n$$\n\nFind the possible values of $\\lambda$ for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above, and write the corresponding Frobenius series forms.\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/example-21897.json" "file_sha256" "18f15c4b4baf3feafb23cf06549f8e1880321934fe778689a804f4d854839046"} {"question" "e-21897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n \\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine the possible values of $\\lambda.$ Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(3\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions have the forms\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $a_n$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069142/example-21897.json" "file_sha256" "18f15c4b4baf3feafb23cf06549f8e1880321934fe778689a804f4d854839046"}] "usage" {"q-342276" [] "q-342074" [] "e-21851" [] "e-21897" []} "basis" 2350 "retractions" [[17592186147728 :question/problem "$x^{2}y^{″} + 2x(x - 2)y^{′} - 4y = 0$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above."] [17592186147729 :question/problem "$4xy^{″} + 2y^{′} - y = 0$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above."] [17592186147171 :question/problem "$x^2y'' + x(x-3)y' - 6y = 0$\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above."] [17592186147171 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nare constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0$, and\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives $y'$ and $y''{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{λ + n})^{′} = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1} \\\\\ny^{″} &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1})^{′} = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} &= x^{2} \\cdot ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\nx(x - 3)y^{′} &= (x^{2} - 3x) \\cdot ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n + 1} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n- 6y &= - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)6a_{n}x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y'$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing\n\n$$\nm=n+1\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nx(x-3)y' = \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty (\\lambda+m-1)a_{m-1}x^{\\lambda+m} - \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 3(\\lambda+n)a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nterm gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3)y^{′} &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n} - 3λa_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n} \\\\\n&=-3λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} - 3(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nterms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} &= (λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n- 6y &= -6a_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)6a_{n}x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nCollecting the terms for\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} - 3λa_{0}x^{λ} - 6a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n(λ^{2} - λ - 3λ - 6)a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n&= (λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0}x^{λ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe obtain an infinite series of the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n\n$$\n\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda)$,\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0}x^{λ} + x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 4λ - 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147173 :question/problem "$12x^2y'' + 7xy' + (2x-3)y = 0$\nFind the possible values of $\\lambda$ for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above, and write the corresponding Frobenius series forms.\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$12x^{2}y^{″}|= 12(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)12((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 7xy^{′}|= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n},; (2x - 3)y|=-3a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(2a_{n - 1} - 3a_{n})x^{λ + n}$.*"] [17592186147173 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nare constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0$, and\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}n=0\n$$\n\nterm isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x^{2}y^{″} &= 12(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)12((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n7xy^{′} &= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n(2x - 3)y &= -3a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(2a_{n - 1} - 3a_{n})x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nCollecting the terms for\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + 7λa_{0}x^{λ} - 3a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n(12λ^{2} - 12λ + 7λ - 3)a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n&= (12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0}x^{λ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe obtain an infinite series of the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n\n$$\n\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda)$,\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0}x^{λ} + x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n(12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0} &= 0 \\\\\n12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine the possible values of $\\lambda$. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(4λ - 3)(3λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= -\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions have the forms\n\n$$\ny(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n - 1/3}andy(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n + 3/4}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $a_n$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases."]] "immutable